    {
        "user": "U05UK5T7LPP",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1737489311.631289",
        "edited": {
            "user": "U05UK5T7LPP",
            "ts": "1737489489.000000"
        },
        "client_msg_id": "b7b5ec30-3c4a-483d-bbab-00595952080a",
        "text": "god, this longread on the software industry hasn't aged in a decade\n<https://www.bloomberg.com/graphics/2015-paul-ford-what-is-code/>\n> Somehow it keeps working out. The industry is always promising to eat itself, to come up with a paradigm so perfect that we can all stop wasting our time and enter a world of pure digital thought. It never happens.\nit's very much a bird's eye view but there's a fantastic balance between the beauty and the ravenous ouroborous\n> Languages are liquid infrastructure. You download a few programs and, whoa, suddenly you have a working Clojure environment. Which is actually the Java Runtime Environment. You grab an old PC that\u2019s outlived its usefulness, put Linux on it, and suddenly you have a powerful Web server.",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "DSU7G",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "god, this longread on the software industry hasn't aged in a decade\n"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "link",
                                "url": "https://www.bloomberg.com/graphics/2015-paul-ford-what-is-code/"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "\n"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_quote",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "Somehow it keeps working out. The industry is always promising to eat itself, to come up with a paradigm so perfect that we can all stop wasting our time and enter a world of pure digital thought. It never happens."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "it's very much a bird's eye view but there's a fantastic balance between the beauty and the ravenous ouroborous\n"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_quote",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "Languages are liquid infrastructure. You download a few programs and, whoa, suddenly you have a working Clojure environment. Which is actually the Java Runtime Environment. You grab an old PC that\u2019s outlived its usefulness, put Linux on it, and suddenly you have a powerful Web server."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ],
